<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Motul de San José</h1><p class="st-meta">600 CE – 850 CE (approx.) · capital Motul de San José</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The seat of the Ik' kingdom near Lake Petén Itzá, famed for the elegant 'Ik'-style' painted vases that portray its courtly life.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Motul de San José lies on the northern side of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-peten-itza">Lake Petén Itzá</a>. Settlement there is older, but the Ik' emblem glyph and its royal court are best known from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-classic-period">Late Classic</a>, from about 600 CE, when the kingdom flourished in the shadow of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tikal">Tikal</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Ik' court is known chiefly through polychrome vases painted in a distinctive style with pink and red tones, showing kings, dancers, tribute presentations and court scenes, some signed by named artists. Its lords were sometimes subordinate to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tikal">Tikal</a> and sometimes rivals. Excavations indicate a modest city with outlying settlements engaged in farming and craft production.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Ik' kingdom declined during the Terminal Classic, and the city was largely abandoned by about 850 CE. Its vases, scattered in museums worldwide, remain a vital source for Maya court life.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/motul-de-san-jose.jpg" alt="Motul de San José" width="320" height="203"><figcaption>Image: Simon Burchell · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Motul_de_San_Jos%C3%A9_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>600 CE – 850 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Motul de San José</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Yajawte' K'inich</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Maya religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Classic Maya</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/guatemala">Guatemala</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ik', Ik' kingdom</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=725&amp;focus=motul-de-san-jose" aria-label="Motul de San José on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2235 1545 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Motul de San José lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2555 1801h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2555 1801h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=725&amp;focus=motul-de-san-jose">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

